q('Which patient is an appropriate routine candidate for C. difficile stool testing?', 'A patient with new otherwise unexplained clinically significant unformed stools',[
 ('An asymptomatic person solely to document colonization','Routine screening is not diagnostic of symptomatic CDI.'),('A recovered patient needing a test of cure despite normal stools','Tests can remain positive and routine cure testing is not recommended.'),('Every patient with a formed stool after one laxative dose','Testing should fit the clinical syndrome and exclude obvious alternative explanations.')],
 'The test must be applied to an appropriate diarrheal syndrome', '**NAAT detects toxigenic-organism genetic potential, not necessarily active toxin-mediated disease.**',['Toxin-based multistep pathways can improve interpretation in suitable testing systems.','Laxatives and other causes of diarrhea must be considered.'], '**Avoid treating colonization as infection.**',['Assess severity and recent antibiotic exposure.','Atypical ileus/fulminant presentations require their own diagnostic approach.'],[['Testing target','Compatible unexplained diarrhea'],['NAAT alone','Can detect colonization'],['After recovery','No routine test of cure']],[CX])
q('For an initial nonfulminant CDI episode in an adult, which treatment is preferred by the 2021 SHEA/IDSA focused update when feasible?', 'Fidaxomicin, with oral vancomycin remaining an acceptable alternative',[
 ('IV vancomycin alone','It does not provide the appropriate luminal treatment.'),('Metronidazole as the preferred agent regardless of access to better options','It is not the preferred first-line option.'),('No treatment if the patient has recently taken an antibiotic','Recent antibiotics are a risk factor, not a reason to withhold treatment.')],
 'Initial clinical cure and recurrence prevention both matter', '**Fidaxomicin is preferred conditionally when resources permit.**',['Oral vancomycin is an accepted alternative.','Fulminant disease has a different treatment pathway.'], '**Review unnecessary precipitating antibiotics.**',['Support fluid and electrolyte needs.','Use infection-control measures and reassess severity.'],[['Preferred if feasible','Fidaxomicin'],['Alternative','Oral vancomycin'],['Separate syndrome','Fulminant CDI']],[CD])
q('A patient with CDI develops shock and ileus. Which regimen principle is appropriate under IDSA/SHEA fulminant-disease guidance?', 'High-dose enteral vancomycin plus IV metronidazole, considering rectal vancomycin when ileus limits delivery',[
 ('Oral fidaxomicin as an automatic evidence-equivalent replacement for every fulminant case','The guideline evidence and recommended fulminant pathway differ.'),('IV vancomycin alone','It does not supply effective luminal treatment.'),('Stop all treatment because diarrhea has diminished during ileus','Reduced stool output can accompany worsening fulminant disease.')],
 'Ileus can hide the severity of toxin-mediated colitis', '**Shock, ileus or megacolon indicates fulminant CDI.**',['Drug delivery to the diseased colon becomes important.','Prompt surgical assessment may be needed.'], '**Treat physiology and the colonic process together.**',['Provide resuscitation and monitor organ function.','Do not wait for profuse diarrhea to recognize deterioration.'],[['Severity','Shock/ileus/megacolon'],['Therapy','Enteral vancomycin plus IV metronidazole'],['Ileus','Consider rectal delivery and urgent team review']],[CD,CX],style='clinical_scenario')
